.TH CLRU_REDECLARE 3 "2026-08-02" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
clru_redeclare \- restore the type-tracking variables for a cache that crosses scopes
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clrucache.h>

clru_redeclare(name, KeyT, ValT);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The type-dispatching macros clru_get() and clru_set() need hidden
companion variables. clru_declare() and clru_construct() create those
variables. When you pass a clru_cache handle to another function, those
companion variables are absent there. clru_redeclare() creates them
again. Every type-inferred macro then works correctly in the new scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name  The existing clru_cache variable that gets the type information
KeyT  Key type. It must match the type of the construction
ValT  Value type. It must match the type of the construction
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Always call clru_redeclare() before you call clru_get() or clru_set() in
a new scope. A missing call is the most common error when a cache
crosses a function boundary.
The name argument must be a simple identifier. The macro pastes it into
the names of the companion variables. It cannot be a different
expression, such as the struct member ga->cache. Copy such a handle into
a local variable. Then redeclare that local variable:

    clru_cache c = ga->cache;
    clru_redeclare(c, int, double);
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void use_cache(clru_cache c) {
    clru_redeclare(c, int, double);
    double out = 0.0;
    if (clru_get(c, 42, &out) == ccol_success) {
        printf("%.2f\en", out);
    }
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clru_declare (3),
.BR clru_construct (3),
.BR clru_get (3),
.BR clru_set (3)
